An array of tuples can hold many records. ReadonlyArray<ScoreRow> describes a list you must not change through this reference.

The array method map calls a function once for each element and collects its returned values into a new array in the same order. For example, amounts.map(amount => amount + 1) returns an incremented value for each amount without changing the original list. For tuple rows, the callback can read row[0] and row[1], or use the destructuring already shown, to form each new label.

```typescript
const amounts = [2, 5];
console.log(amounts.map(amount => amount + 1));
console.log(amounts);
```
The arrays displayed are [3, 6] and [2, 5]. The callback receives each amount; map collects its returned numbers into a different array.

For each required name, find its result. Keep the name in the blocked list if it is missing, not successful, or belongs to another revision.

results.find(item => item.name === wantedName) returns the first matching result, or undefined when none matches. Check for that missing value before reading its status or revision. filter keeps each required name for which your blocking condition is true.

```javascript
const results = [{name:"tests",status:"success"}];
console.log(results.find(item => item.name === "tests").status);
console.log(results.find(item => item.name === "build"));
console.log(["tests", "build"].filter(name => name === "build"));
```
The outputs are success, undefined, and ["build"]. find returns the first matching element or undefined; filter returns a new array of all matching elements. Each receives a predicate callback and leaves the source array unchanged. These calls show selection only; the full exercise also checks revision and status.

The exercise's list uses Array.from({length: 1000}, (_, i) => `Book ${i}`). It creates indexes 0 through 999; _ is a parameter name for an unused value and i is the index. Book 99 matches Book 99 and Book 990 through Book 999: 11 matches. slice(0, 10) displays the first ten without changing the full match array. Check every matching value as well as equal lengths: equal counts alone can hide different results.

```javascript
const matches = ["A", "B", "C"];
console.log(matches.slice(0, 2));
console.log(matches);
```
The arrays are ["A", "B"] and ["A", "B", "C"]. slice receives a start index and an excluded end index, returning a new shallow array without changing matches.

The exercise has add and toggle actions. Add creates an unfinished task unless its ID already exists. Toggle changes only the matching task. An unknown action keeps the current state.

some checks whether any array entry passes a condition: tasks.some(task => task.id === wantedId) answers whether that ID is already present. To append without editing the old array, [...tasks, newTask] creates a new array containing its old entries followed by the new task.

```javascript
const tasks = [{id:"read"}, {id:"draw"}];
console.log(tasks.some(task => task.id === "read"));
console.log(tasks.some(task => task.id === "walk"));
```
This prints true, then false. some receives a predicate and returns true once an entry passes; it returns false if none pass, including an empty array. It does not change tasks.

The exercise also puts returned greetings on a page. In a browser, `document` represents the page. `document.querySelector("#g1")` finds the element with id g1; the `#` marks an id in this selector string. Assigning its `.textContent` sets its plain text. That requires the supplied HTML elements to exist. It does not treat the greeting as HTML instructions, so angle brackets in text stay text.

In a browser page containing `<p id="g1"></p>`, run:
```javascript
const output = document.querySelector("#g1");
output.textContent = "Hi, Noor!";
console.log(output.textContent);
```
This displays Hi, Noor!. querySelector receives a CSS selector string and returns the first matching element, or null if none exists. This example supplies the matching element before accessing textContent; code for an uncertain page must check for null.

A timestamp records when something happened. JavaScript's `Date.now()` gives a timestamp measured in milliseconds since a fixed reference date. A millisecond is one thousandth of a second. To measure a duration, subtract the earlier timestamp from the later one. The result is elapsed time, not a clock reading. Both numbers must use the same unit and reference.

```javascript
const recorded = Date.now();
console.log(typeof recorded);
console.log(Number.isFinite(recorded));
```
This prints number and true. Date.now takes no arguments and returns milliseconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z. Its exact number changes with the wall clock, so the example checks the result kind rather than promising a fixed timestamp.

A dependency is code from another package that your project uses. The supplied local example uses Lodash's sum function. `require("lodash")` names an installed package rather than a neighboring file, and `const { sum }` extracts its exported function. The package.json file records project information and a dependencies object. The supplied version range begins with `^`, allowing compatible updates within that major version; a lockfile records resolved versions more precisely.

In the local CommonJS project after Lodash is installed:
```javascript
const { sum } = require("lodash");
console.log(sum([12, 18, 5]));
console.log(sum([]));
```
This prints 35, then 0. Here sum receives an array of numbers and returns their total without changing that array. This package call belongs to the local Node project, not the browser exercise.
